    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Buying a house in a flood zone in Houston can work out fine, but only if you check the risk before your option period ends. That means reading the FEMA flood zone, studying the seller's flood disclosures, getting a flood insurance quote and, ideally, seeing an elevation certificate. Flood insurance can take 30 days to start, so line it up with your closing rather than leaving it to the last week.</p>
<p>Here is how Houston buyers can size up flood risk and protect themselves from contract to closing.</p>

<h2>Understanding FEMA flood zones in Houston</h2>
<p>FEMA's Flood Insurance Rate Maps (FIRMs) divide land into zones by flood risk. The labels show up on disclosures, lender paperwork and insurance quotes, so it pays to know what they mean.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Zone</th><th>What it means</th><th>Flood insurance with a federally backed mortgage</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>A, AE, AH, AO, AR, A99</td><td>Special Flood Hazard Area, the "100-year floodplain," with a 1% annual chance of flooding</td><td>Required</td></tr>
<tr><td>V, VE</td><td>Coastal high hazard area with wave action, mostly along the Gulf coast</td><td>Required</td></tr>
<tr><td>X (shaded)</td><td>Moderate risk, the "500-year floodplain," with a 0.2% annual chance of flooding</td><td>Optional, recommended</td></tr>
<tr><td>X (unshaded)</td><td>Minimal mapped risk</td><td>Optional, still worth pricing</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>A "1% annual chance" sounds small, but it adds up over a mortgage. And maps only show modeled risk: homes outside the mapped floodplain have flooded in past Houston storms, often from heavy rain overwhelming streets and drainage rather than a bayou overtopping its banks.</p>
<p>You can look up the effective map for any address on the <a href="https://msc.fema.gov/portal/home">FEMA Flood Map Service Center</a>. If the home sits in or next to a floodway, where water moves fastest, expect tighter building rules and higher insurance costs.</p>

<h2>Harris County flood tools to check before you buy</h2>
<p>Harris County gives buyers better local data than most places. Check these during your option period:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>MAAPnext.</strong> The Harris County Flood Control District's <a href="https://maapnext.harriscountytx.gov/">MAAPnext site</a> lets you search an address and see draft flood risk information built on newer rainfall and elevation data, including NOAA's Atlas 14 rainfall study. Draft FEMA maps for Harris County were shared in 2026, and some neighborhoods are moving into the floodplain while others are moving out.</li>
<li><strong>Harris County Flood Education Mapping Tool.</strong> Linked from the MAAPnext resources page, it is another way to view flood information for a neighborhood.</li>
<li><strong>Harris County Flood Warning System.</strong> Rain and stream gauge data show how nearby channels respond during storms.</li>
</ul>
<p>Keep in mind that the draft maps are for information only. They are not open for formal appeals and cannot be used for insurance or permitting decisions until FEMA finalizes them. Still, if a home you like is shown moving into a higher-risk zone, factor that into your price and insurance budget now, because future buyers will see the same map.</p>

<h2>What the Texas seller's disclosure tells you about flooding</h2>
<p>Texas Property Code Section 5.008 requires most sellers of single-family homes to give buyers a Seller's Disclosure Notice. Since a 2019 law expanded the flood section, sellers have had to answer specific flood questions, and the <a href="https://www.trec.texas.gov/forms/sellers-disclosure-notice">current TREC Seller's Disclosure Notice</a> (TREC No. 55-1, updated in 2026) keeps them. The seller must say whether they are aware of:</p>
<ul>
<li>Present flood insurance coverage.</li>
<li>Previous flooding due to a failure or breach of a reservoir, or a controlled or emergency release of water from a reservoir.</li>
<li>Previous water penetration into a structure on the property due to a natural flood event.</li>
<li>Whether the property is wholly or partly in a 100-year floodplain, a 500-year floodplain, a floodway, a flood pool or a reservoir.</li>
</ul>
<p>The form also asks two yes-or-no questions that are especially useful: whether the seller has ever filed a flood damage claim with any insurance provider, including the National Flood Insurance Program, and whether the seller has ever received assistance from FEMA or the U.S. Small Business Administration for flood damage. Updates in 2026 also added a question on whether the seller has been unable to insure the property for any reason.</p>
<p>Under the TREC resale contract, if the seller delivers the notice after the contract's effective date, you may terminate for any reason within 7 days after you receive it or before closing, whichever comes first, and get your earnest money back. Read it the day it arrives. For the full picture of what Texas sellers must disclose, see our guide to the <a href="/blog/texas-seller-disclosure-notice/">Texas seller disclosure notice</a>.</p>

<h3>Flood disclosures for Houston rentals and investors</h3>
<p>If you plan to rent the home out, Texas has required a landlord flood disclosure since January 1, 2022. Under Property Code Section 92.0135, landlords must tell tenants in writing whether they know the dwelling is in a 100-year floodplain and whether it has flooded at least once in the last five years. A landlord who skips it can face a tenant ending the lease after a flood loss, so build this into your rental paperwork from day one.</p>

<h2>Questions to ask before buying a house in a flood zone in Houston</h2>
<ol>
<li>Has the home or garage ever taken on water, and how high?</li>
<li>Were any flood claims paid, and were repairs completed with permits?</li>
<li>Is there an existing elevation certificate or survey showing the finished floor height?</li>
<li>Does the seller carry flood insurance now, and what does it cost?</li>
<li>Has the home been elevated, or have drainage or detention improvements been made nearby?</li>
<li>How did the street handle major past storms?</li>
</ol>
<p>Ask neighbors too. They will often tell you which streets hold water, which is information no map captures.</p>

<h2>Flood insurance: NFIP vs private</h2>
<p>Standard homeowners policies do not cover flood damage, so flood coverage is a separate policy. In Houston you will choose between the federal National Flood Insurance Program and private carriers.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>NFIP</th><th>Private flood</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Max building coverage (residential)</td><td>$250,000</td><td>Often higher, varies by carrier</td></tr>
<tr><td>Max contents coverage</td><td>$100,000</td><td>Varies</td></tr>
<tr><td>Waiting period</td><td>Generally 30 days, with exceptions</td><td>Set by the carrier, often shorter</td></tr>
<tr><td>Additional living expenses</td><td>Not covered</td><td>Sometimes included</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pricing</td><td>Risk Rating 2.0, property-specific</td><td>Carrier underwriting</td></tr>
<tr><td>Availability</td><td>Available in participating communities</td><td>Carriers can decline or non-renew</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Federally regulated lenders must accept private flood policies that meet federal requirements, so private coverage can satisfy a mortgage requirement. The trade-off is stability: NFIP coverage stays available in participating communities, while a private carrier can decide not to renew. Ask whether the seller's NFIP policy can be assigned to you at closing, and compare that quote against a new policy.</p>
[[product:quick-dam-flood-barrier]]

<h3>The 30-day waiting period and your closing</h3>
<p>NFIP policies generally take effect 30 days after purchase. For buyers, the key exception is that the wait does not apply when flood insurance is purchased in connection with making a mortgage loan, as long as you apply at or before closing. Cash buyers do not get that exception, so if you are paying cash, buy the policy as early as possible. Private carriers set their own waiting periods, so ask before you rely on one.</p>

<h2>Elevation certificates: why they still matter</h2>
<p>An elevation certificate is a FEMA form completed by a licensed surveyor, engineer or architect. It records the building's elevation compared with the base flood elevation, plus details such as the lowest floor and machinery like the A/C unit.</p>
<p>Under FEMA's Risk Rating 2.0, an elevation certificate is optional for NFIP pricing because FEMA can use its own elevation data. Submitting one can lower your premium, but it does not always, so ask your agent to run the quote both ways. Ask the seller if they have one, and check with the community floodplain office, which may keep certificates on file for newer or elevated homes.</p>
<p>Elevation also matters for remodeling. Inside Houston city limits, the 2018 revision to Chapter 19 requires new construction and substantial improvements in the 100-year and 500-year floodplains to sit two feet above the 500-year flood elevation. If you plan a major renovation or addition on a low slab home, confirm the rules with Houston Public Works before you buy.</p>

<h2>Protecting your investment after closing</h2>
<ul>
<li>Keep flood insurance in force every year, even if your lender does not require it.</li>
<li>Store your policy, elevation certificate and closing documents in a waterproof bag you can grab quickly.</li>
<li>Photograph every room and major item to speed up any future claim.</li>
<li>Clean gutters and storm drains near your home before hurricane season and big storms.</li>
<li>Use our <a href="/tools/hurricane-prep-checklist/">hurricane prep checklist</a> each spring.</li>
</ul>
<p>Flood risk also affects your monthly budget. Add the flood premium to your payment estimate, and plan for flood insurance in your prepaids, which our guide to <a href="/blog/closing-costs-in-texas/">closing costs in Texas</a> explains. This article is general information, so confirm coverage details with a licensed insurance agent and contract questions with a real estate attorney.</p>

<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>Buying a house in a flood zone in Houston is a numbers decision, not a dealbreaker by default. Check the FEMA zone and MAAPnext drafts, read every flood answer on the seller's disclosure, get a real flood insurance quote with and without an elevation certificate, and bind coverage at or before closing. If the risk and the cost still fit, you can buy with confidence.</p>
